<p class="lede">Current ASHRAE guidance accepts much drier data center air than older practice did. Research sponsored by ASHRAE found that, with ordinary grounding and ESD practices, low humidity does not meaningfully raise the risk of electrostatic damage to equipment, so lower humidity limits were adopted. High humidity, especially combined with pollutants, is now considered the bigger threat.</p>

<h2>The old practice and why it changed</h2>
<p>For years, data centers held relative humidity around 40% to 55%, with humidifiers running for much of the winter. The reasoning was that dry air allows static charge to build up on people and objects, and that a discharge into equipment could damage components. Humidifying large rooms, particularly with outdoor air economizers in cold climates, used a great deal of energy and water.</p>
<p>ASHRAE Technical Committee 9.9 sponsored research into electrostatic discharge at low humidity, testing the charge generated by people walking on typical data center floors in various footwear and flooring combinations. The findings showed that when floors and equipment are properly grounded and staff follow basic ESD practices, the risk of damaging discharges at low humidity is small. Successive editions of the thermal guidelines lowered the minimum moisture limit to reflect this, which allowed operators to reduce or eliminate humidification.</p>

<h2>What the guidance says now</h2>
<p>The current thermal guidelines express the lower moisture limit as a dew point, together with a minimum relative humidity, rather than as RH alone. Dew point is a better measure of absolute moisture because it does not change as air warms across the servers. The recommended envelope's lower limit is a dew point a little below freezing; allowable classes go lower, to very dry conditions. Exact values and the full class definitions are in <a href="<?= url('guides/ashrae-tc-9-9-thermal-guidelines') ?>">ASHRAE TC 9.9 thermal guidelines for data centers</a>; confirm the edition the owner has adopted.</p>
<p class="note">Specify humidity limits as dew point where possible. A room at 75°F and 20% RH is the same air as 95°F hot aisle air at about 11% RH; RH alone gives two different numbers for one moisture content.</p>

<h2>Conditions that keep low humidity safe</h2>
<p>The reduced ESD risk depends on the facility following practices that limit charge generation and give charge a safe path to ground:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Grounded flooring.</strong> Raised floor tiles and slab finishes with defined electrical resistance to ground, so charge does not accumulate on people walking on them.</li>
<li><strong>Grounded racks and equipment.</strong> Racks bonded to the facility grounding system.</li>
<li><strong>Personnel practices.</strong> Wrist straps or ESD footwear when handling components or working inside servers, especially when installing or replacing parts.</li>
<li><strong>Limited insulating materials.</strong> Avoid carpets, plastic sheeting and packing materials in the white space that generate charge.</li>
<li><strong>Component handling.</strong> Unpack and handle boards and drives in designated ESD-safe areas.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where those practices cannot be assured, such as in mixed-use rooms or older facilities with carpet, the owner may choose a higher humidity floor.</p>

<h2>Why high humidity is the bigger concern</h2>
<p>Moisture contributes to several failure mechanisms on electronics:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Corrosion</strong> of copper and silver on circuit boards and connectors accelerates as humidity rises, especially when sulfur or chlorine compounds are present.</li>
<li><strong>Dust deliquescence:</strong> some dust particles contain salts that absorb moisture and become conductive above a threshold relative humidity, creating leakage paths between closely spaced conductors.</li>
<li><strong>Conductive filament growth</strong> inside circuit boards under moisture and voltage stress.</li>
</ul>
<p>For this reason, the guidelines set upper limits by both dew point and RH, and recent editions recommend a lower upper RH limit in environments with elevated gaseous pollutants. Testing for those pollutants is described in <a href="<?= url('guides/gaseous-contamination-isa-71-04') ?>">gaseous contamination in data centers: ISA 71.04</a>.</p>

<h2>Implications for air handler design</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Design element</th><th>Effect of current guidance</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Humidifiers</td><td>Often eliminated, or sized only for extreme dry conditions in direct economizer systems</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dehumidification</td><td>Still needed in humid climates with outdoor air; dew point high limit governs economizer use</td></tr>
<tr><td>Economizer hours</td><td>Cold, dry outdoor air becomes usable without humidification, increasing hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sensors</td><td>Dew point or high-accuracy RH with temperature, calibrated regularly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water use</td><td>Reduced by avoiding humidification</td></tr>
<tr><td>Liquid cooling</td><td>Room dew point must stay below cold surfaces; see <a href="<?= url('guides/liquid-cooling-residual-air-load') ?>">liquid cooling and the residual air load</a></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>In a closed recirculating data hall, moisture changes slowly: the only sources are infiltration, people and makeup air. Many such halls operate with no humidifier at all and simply monitor dew point. In a direct airside economizer system, outdoor air sets the moisture level, and the controls must decide what to do when outdoor dew point falls below the chosen lower limit: humidify, reduce outdoor air, or accept the condition. The economizer side of that decision is in <a href="<?= url('guides/airside-economizer-data-centers') ?>">airside economizers for data centers</a>.</p>

<h2>Setting a humidity policy</h2>
<ol>
<li>Choose the envelope (recommended or a specific allowable class) and record its dew point and RH limits.</li>
<li>Confirm ESD controls in the white space: flooring, grounding, procedures.</li>
<li>Assess gaseous contamination; lower the upper RH limit if pollutants are elevated.</li>
<li>Decide whether humidification will be installed and at what dew point it activates.</li>
<li>Define sensor type, location (return or supply, and at rack inlets) and calibration interval.</li>
</ol>
